Lab 16: Customizing Camel Routes for High Availability
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure Apache Camel routes in a high-availability (HA) environment.
Implement load balancing and failover strategies for fault tolerance.
Use persistent messaging to prevent data loss.
Prerequisites
Before starting, ensure you have:

Basic knowledge of Apache Camel and Java.
A working OpenJDK (version 11 or later) installed.
Apache Camel (latest version) set up in your development environment.
A message broker (e.g., Apache ActiveMQ or RabbitMQ) for persistent messaging.
Access to a Kubernetes/OpenShift cluster (optional, for HA testing).
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Set Up Clustered Camel Routes in an HA Environment
Objective: Configure Camel routes in a clustered environment for high availability.

Subtask 1.1: Install and Configure Apache Camel
Install Apache Camel using Maven by adding the following dependency to your pom.xml:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
    <artifactId>camel-core</artifactId>
    <version>3.20.0</version>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
    <artifactId>camel-quarkus</artifactId>
    <version>3.20.0</version>
</dependency>
Create a basic Camel route in a Java class:

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class HighAvailabilityRoute extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        from("timer:haTimer?period=5000")
            .log("High Availability Route: Message Sent");
    }
}
Run the route in a clustered environment (e.g., Kubernetes/OpenShift) by deploying multiple instances.

Expected Outcome:

The Camel route runs in multiple instances, ensuring redundancy.
Task 2: Implement Load Balancing and Failover Strategies
Objective: Use Camel’s load balancing and failover mechanisms for fault tolerance.

Subtask 2.1: Configure Load Balancing
Modify the route to include a load balancer:

from("direct:start")
    .loadBalance()
        .roundRobin()
        .to("seda:route1", "seda:route2");
Define secondary routes:

from("seda:route1")
    .log("Processing in Route 1");

from("seda:route2")
    .log("Processing in Route 2");
Subtask 2.2: Implement Failover
Use Camel’s failover mechanism:

from("direct:start")
    .onException(Exception.class)
        .maximumRedeliveries(3)
        .redeliveryDelay(1000)
    .end()
    .to("direct:fallback");
Define a fallback route:

from("direct:fallback")
    .log("Fallback route activated due to failure");
Expected Outcome:

Messages are distributed evenly across routes.
If a route fails, the system retries and falls back to a secondary route.
Task 3: Implement Persistent Messaging
Objective: Ensure no data loss by using persistent message storage.

Subtask 3.1: Configure Apache ActiveMQ for Persistence
Install ActiveMQ and add the Camel JMS dependency:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
    <artifactId>camel-jms</artifactId>
    <version>3.20.0</version>
</dependency>
Configure a persistent JMS endpoint:

from("jms:queue:orders?transacted=true")
    .to("log:PersistentMessage");
Enable persistence in ActiveMQ (activemq.xml):

<persistenceAdapter>
    <kahaDB directory="${activemq.data}/kahadb"/>
</persistenceAdapter>
Expected Outcome:

Messages are stored persistently in ActiveMQ and survive broker restarts.
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Configured high-availability Camel routes in a clustered environment.
Implemented load balancing and failover for fault tolerance.
Ensured persistent messaging to prevent data loss.
By following these steps, you can build resilient Camel applications that maintain uptime and reliability in production environments.

Troubleshooting Tips
ActiveMQ not starting? Check activemq.log for errors.
Routes not balancing? Verify all endpoints are correctly defined.
Persistence issues? Ensure the kahadb directory has write permissions.
